</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::arms_transfer_volume" target="global_ai_race_and_gover::security_incident_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased transfers of weapons and autonomous systems (drones, guided munitions) into the theatre multiplies the number of deployed AI-enabled platforms that can be captured, spoofed, exploited, or malfunction, raising the frequency of security incidents.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_ai_race_and_gover</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::arms_transfer_volume" target="global_energy_supply_sho::geo_political_tension" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Larger and more visible arms deliveries to one side prompt retaliatory rhetoric, countermeasures and security posturing by opposing states, directly raising international political and security tensions that affect energy risk perceptions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::arms_transfer_volume" target="middle_east_iran_houthis::kinetic_attack_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased volumes of arms transfers tied to the Russia‑Ukraine conflict (commercial or state-level diversions and barter deals) raise the flow of drones, cruise‑missile components and munitions into the region, enabling Iran‑aligned groups like the Houthis to mount more frequent and higher‑payload kinetic strikes.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">middle_east_iran_houthis</data>
