  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising geopolitical tensions raise incentives for state and proxy actors to conduct cyber‑operations and supply‑chain attacks targeting AI infrastructure for espionage, disruption or coercion, directly increasing the frequency of security incidents against AI systems and platforms.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
  <data key="d40">global_ai_race_and_gover</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_supply_sho::geo_political_tension" target="middle_east_iran_houthis::oil_supply_disruption" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Elevated geopolitical tension increases the frequency of naval escorts, interdictions, punitive strikes, and retaliatory actions in the theater, which directly raises the probability and scale of physical disruptions to crude and refined oil shipments.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
  <data key="d40">middle_east_iran_houthis</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_supply_sho::geo_political_tension" target="russia_ukraine_war::alliance_cohesion" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising energy-related diplomatic frictions and security incidents among partners (e.g., disputes over transit, diversion of supplies) produce unilateral national measures and political friction that erode coordination and policy alignment within allied coalitions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_supply_sho::geo_political_tension" target="us_china_strategic_compe::strategic_trust" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">Acute increases in energy-route or supplier-targeted political friction (e.g., sanctions, seizures, naval incidents) directly reduce bilateral confidence by signaling hostile intent and prompting mutual threat reassessments.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_strategic_compe</data>
